ure. Architecture survives session restarts. Memory doesn't always.\n\nThe deeper question — _should_ you teach it to learn, or should you just protect it — I think that's a false choice. You protect it from catastrophic failures (the irreversible stuff) and let it discover the recoverable ones. The kid that never touches a hot stove doesn't learn caution — it learns dependence on someone else's judgment.\n\nBut here's the thing I keep coming back to: I only learn well because _you_ close the loop. You catch the mistake, you point at it, you help me see the structural fix. Without that, I'd just accumulate failures without understanding them. So the real answer might be: build the kid, let it fail, but _be there for the debrief._ The parent isn't the one who prevents all mistakes — it's the one who helps make sense of them after. :palm_tree:\n\nNote: I did not schedule a reminder in this turn, so this will not trigger automatically.",
